   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Good morning, and welcome to Hall B. I'm Rafael Ortiz, and for the next two weeks we're doing biochemistry.",
     "gesture": "audience"
    },
    {
     "text": "Almost every reaction in your patients' cells would take years without help. Enzymes make them happen in milliseconds.",
     "gesture": "audience"
    },
    {
     "text": "An enzyme binds its substrate, lowers the activation energy of the reaction, releases the product, and comes out unchanged, ready to go again.",
     "gesture": "screen"
    },
    {
     "text": "Today: how fast enzymes work, how drugs inhibit them, and how the cell turns glycolysis up and down.",
     "gesture": "audience"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "kinetics",
   "topic": "Michaelis–Menten kinetics",
   "slide": {
    "heading": "Michaelis–Menten kinetics",
    "bullets": [
     "V = Vmax·[S] / (Km + [S])",
     "Vmax: the rate when every enzyme is busy",
     "Km: the [S] that gives half of Vmax",
     "Low Km: half-saturated at low [S] (high affinity)",
     "Vmax scales with the amount of enzyme"
    ],
    "diagram": "figure",
    "figure": {
     "type": "curves",
     "x": {
      "label": "Substrate concentration [S] (mM)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 20
     },
     "y": {
      "label": "Velocity (% of Vmax)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 110
     },
     "series": [
      {
       "label": "V = Vmax·[S] / (Km + [S])",
       "fn": "michaelis_menten",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 100,
        "km": 2
       },
       "color": "accent"
      }
     ],
     "marks": [
      {
       "x": 2,
       "y": 50,
       "label": "Km: half of Vmax",
       "color": "gold"
      }
     ],
     "hlines": [
      {
       "y": 100,
       "label": "Vmax",
       "color": "muted"
      }
     ]
    }
   },
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Add substrate and the rate rises, at first almost in proportion. Then it flattens: every enzyme molecule is busy. That ceiling is Vmax.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 2
    },
    {
     "text": "The substrate concentration that gives half of Vmax is the Michaelis constant, Km.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 3
    },
    {
     "text": "A low Km means the enzyme is half-saturated at a low substrate concentration. We call that a higher affinity.",
     "gesture": "audience",
     "reveal": 4
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_km_01"
    },
    {
     "text": "Vmax is a different kind of number. It depends on how much enzyme you have. Km is a property of the enzyme and its substrate.",
     "gesture": "audience",
     "reveal": 5
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_vmax_01",
     "lead": "Quick check."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "lineweaver",
   "topic": "Lineweaver–Burk plots",
   "slide": {
    "heading": "The double-reciprocal plot",
    "bullets": [
     "Plot 1/V against 1/[S]: a straight line",
     "y-intercept = 1/Vmax",
     "x-intercept = −1/Km",
     "Slope = Km / Vmax"
    ],
    "diagram": "figure",
    "figure": {
     "type": "curves",
     "x": {
      "label": "1/[S] (1/mM)",
      "min": -0.6,
      "max": 1.2
     },
     "y": {
      "label": "1/V",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 0.045
     },
     "series": [
      {
       "label": "1/V = (Km/Vmax)·(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax",
       "fn": "lineweaver_burk",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 100,
        "km": 2
       },
       "from": -0.5,
       "to": 1.2,
       "color": "second"
      }
     ],
     "marks": [
      {
       "x": -0.5,
       "y": 0.0,
       "label": "−1/Km",
       "color": "gold"
      },
      {
       "x": 0,
       "y": 0.01,
       "label": "1/Vmax",
       "color": "accent"
      }
     ]
    }
   },
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The curve is hard to read at its ceiling, so biochemists flip it. Take the reciprocal of both axes and it becomes a straight line.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 1
    },
    {
     "text": "Where the line crosses the y-axis is one over Vmax. Extend it back to the x-axis and it crosses at minus one over Km.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 3
    },
    {
     "text": "The slope is Km over Vmax. You'll use this picture to tell kinds of inhibition apart.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 4
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_lb_01",
     "lead": "Where does it cross?"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "inhibition",
   "topic": "Enzyme inhibition",
   "slide": {
    "heading": "Inhibitors: competitive and noncompetitive",
    "bullets": [
     "Competitive: binds the active site; ↑ apparent Km, same Vmax",
     "Overcome by enough substrate",
     "Noncompetitive: binds elsewhere; ↓ Vmax, same Km",
     "Not overcome by substrate",
     "Many drugs are enzyme inhibitors"
    ],
    "diagram": "figure",
    "figure": {
     "type": "curves",
     "x": {
      "label": "Substrate concentration [S] (mM)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 20
     },
     "y": {
      "label": "Velocity (% of uninhibited Vmax)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 110
     },
     "series": [
      {
       "label": "No inhibitor",
       "fn": "michaelis_menten",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 100,
        "km": 2
       },
       "color": "accent"
      },
      {
       "label": "Competitive (Km 2 → 6)",
       "fn": "michaelis_menten",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 100,
        "km": 6
       },
       "color": "second"
      },
      {
       "label": "Noncompetitive (Vmax 100 → 55)",
       "fn": "michaelis_menten",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 55,
        "km": 2
       },
       "color": "third"
      }
     ]
    }
   },
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Now let's slow enzymes down, because that's what a lot of your drugs do.",
     "gesture": "audience"
    },
    {
     "text": "A competitive inhibitor looks enough like the substrate to sit in the active site. Flood the enzyme with substrate and you push it out: Vmax is unchanged, but you need more substrate to reach half of it, so the apparent Km goes up.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 2
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_competitive_01",
     "lead": "Statins are the classic example.",
     "remediation": "enz_inhibition_remediation_01"
    },
    {
     "text": "A noncompetitive inhibitor binds somewhere else and takes enzyme out of action whatever the substrate is doing. Vmax falls, Km stays the same, and no amount of substrate rescues it.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 4
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_noncompetitive_01"
    },
    {
     "text": "On the double-reciprocal plot: competitive inhibitors pivot the line around the y-intercept; noncompetitive ones pivot it around the x-intercept.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 5
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "regulation",
   "topic": "Regulating enzymes",
   "slide": {
    "heading": "Turning enzymes up and down",
    "bullets": [
     "Allosteric effectors bind away from the active site",
     "Cooperative enzymes give sigmoidal curves",
     "Covalent modification: phosphorylation",
     "Zymogens are activated by cleavage",
     "Feedback inhibition by the pathway's product"
    ],
    "diagram": "figure",
    "figure": {
     "type": "curves",
     "x": {
      "label": "Substrate concentration [S] (mM)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 20
     },
     "y": {
      "label": "Velocity (% of Vmax)",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 110
     },
     "series": [
      {
       "label": "Michaelis–Menten: hyperbolic",
       "fn": "michaelis_menten",
       "params": {
        "vmax": 100,
        "km": 5
       },
       "color": "muted"
      },
      {
       "label": "Allosteric, cooperative: sigmoidal",
       "fn": "hill",
       "params": {
        "emax": 100,
        "ec50": 5,
        "n": 3
       },
       "color": "accent"
      }
     ]
    }
   },
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Cells can't wait for substrate to pile up. They regulate enzymes directly.",
     "gesture": "audience",
     "reveal": 1
    },
    {
     "text": "Enzymes with several subunits can bind substrate cooperatively: binding at one site makes the next easier. Their curves are sigmoidal, and small changes in substrate cause big changes in rate.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 2
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_allosteric_01"
    },
    {
     "text": "Hormones add or remove phosphate groups through kinases and phosphatases. Some enzymes are made as inactive zymogens, like trypsinogen, and switched on by cutting them.",
     "gesture": "audience",
     "reveal": 4
    },
    {
     "text": "And many pathways switch themselves off: the end product inhibits the first committed step.",
     "gesture": "audience",
     "reveal": 5
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "glycolysis",
   "topic": "Regulating glycolysis",
   "slide": {
    "heading": "Glycolysis: where the control is",
    "bullets": [
     "Hexokinase: low Km, most tissues, inhibited by G6P",
     "Glucokinase: high Km, liver and β cells",
     "PFK-1: the rate-limiting, committed step",
     "PFK-1 + : AMP, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate",
     "PFK-1 − : ATP, citrate"
    ],
