   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Let's put it together in glycolysis, which your patients' red cells run on entirely.",
     "gesture": "audience"
    },
    {
     "text": "The first step traps glucose in the cell. Most tissues use hexokinase: a low Km, and inhibited by its own product.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 1
    },
    {
     "text": "The liver and the pancreatic β cells use glucokinase: a high Km, so it works in proportion to how much glucose is around. That makes it a glucose sensor.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 2
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_glucokinase_01"
    },
    {
     "text": "The committed, rate-limiting step is phosphofructokinase-1. Signals of low energy, AMP, and of the fed state, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, switch it on. Plenty of ATP and citrate switch it off.",
     "gesture": "screen",
     "reveal": 5
    },
    {
     "question": "enz_pfk1_01",
     "lead": "Which one turns it on?"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
